  "titleEn": "Too Old For This Shit",
  "titleEs": "Demasiado viejo para esta mierda",
  "titleText": {
    "es": "Dicen que si un matemático no ha publicado su gran obra a la edad de 11 años, nunca lo hará.",
    "en": "They say if a mathematician doesn't do their great work by age eleven, they never will.",
    "noteEn": "The title text extends the joke by moving the supposed deadline for a mathematician's major work down to age eleven. The Spanish archive phrases the achievement specifically as publishing the great work and prints 11 as digits."
  },
  "cardDefinitions": [
    {
      "id": "word-darle--engage-in-activity",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "darle",
      "answerEn": "to do; to be into an activity (with a)",
      "noteEn": "In the colloquial pattern darle a una actividad, darle means to do that activity, practice it, or spend time on it. The attached le is part of the expression rather than a separately named recipient.",
      "example": { "es": "Alicia le da al ajedrez.", "en": "Alicia is into chess." },
      "tags": ["word", "darle a", "activity", "colloquial", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a--fixed-darle-a-activity",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; links darle to an activity",
      "noteEn": "This a is required in the colloquial pattern darle a una actividad. It introduces the activity that someone does or practices.",
      "example": { "es": "Le damos a la fotografía.", "en": "We are into photography." },
      "tags": ["word", "darle a", "fixed link", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-me--resultar-experiencer",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "me",
      "answerEn": "to me; marks me as the person who finds something a certain way",
      "noteEn": "With resultar plus a description, me identifies the speaker as the person experiencing that judgment. Natural English often makes that person the subject: me resulta difícil is commonly expressed as “I find it difficult.”",
      "example": { "es": "La tarea me resulta sencilla.", "en": "I find the task simple." },
      "tags": ["word", "experiencer", "resultar", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-resulta--seems-to-person",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "resulta",
      "answerEn": "seems; feels; is found to be (by someone)",
      "noteEn": "Resulta is a present form of resultar for one thing. With me, te, le, nos, os, or les and a description, it says how that thing seems or feels to a person.",
      "example": { "es": "El ejercicio me resulta fácil.", "en": "I find the exercise easy." },
      "tags": ["word", "resultar", "evaluation", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-antes--formerly",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "antes",
      "answerEn": "before; earlier; in the past",
      "noteEn": "Used by itself, antes can point back to an earlier time rather than introducing a following event.",
      "example": { "es": "Antes vivíamos cerca.", "en": "We used to live nearby." },
      "tags": ["word", "time", "past", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-ajá--acknowledgment",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "ajá",
      "answerEn": "uh-huh; I see",
      "noteEn": "Ajá is a short acknowledgment showing that someone is following or has understood what was said. Tone can also make it skeptical.",
      "example": { "es": "—Llegamos a las seis. —Ajá.", "en": "“We arrive at six.” “Uh-huh.”" },
      "tags": ["word", "interjection", "acknowledgment", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-matemática",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "matemática",
      "answerEn": "mathematics; math",
      "noteEn": "The singular matemática can name mathematics as a field. The plural matemáticas is also very common for the subject or activity.",
      "example": { "es": "La matemática estudia patrones.", "en": "Mathematics studies patterns." },
      "tags": ["word", "mathematics", "academic field", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-es--classification",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "es",
      "answerEn": "is; belongs to the stated category",
      "noteEn": "Es is the present form of ser used for one person or thing. Between two naming words, it identifies or classifies the first as the second.",
      "example": { "es": "El ajedrez es un juego.", "en": "Chess is a game." },
      "tags": ["word", "ser", "classification", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-jóvenes--young-people",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "jóvenes",
      "answerEn": "young people",
      "noteEn": "A describing word such as joven can stand for people when the person-word is understood. Los jóvenes means young people as a group.",
      "example": { "es": "Los jóvenes organizaron el evento.", "en": "The young people organized the event." },
      "tags": ["word", "people", "plural", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-echarme--step-aside",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "echarme",
      "answerEn": "to step aside; to move myself out of the way (with a un lado)",
      "noteEn": "Echarme combines the unchanged verb echar with me. In echarme a un lado, the full expression means that the speaker steps aside or gets out of the way.",
      "example": { "es": "Voy a echarme a un lado.", "en": "I am going to step aside." },
      "tags": ["word", "echarse a un lado", "attached pronoun", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a--fixed-echarse-a-un-lado",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; part of echarse a un lado",
      "noteEn": "This a is the fixed link between echarse and un lado in the expression for stepping aside.",
      "example": { "es": "Nos echamos a un lado.", "en": "We stepped aside." },
      "tags": ["word", "echarse a un lado", "fixed link", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-lado",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "lado",
      "answerEn": "side",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "position", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-dar--make-way",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "dar",
      "answerEn": "to give way; to make way (with paso a)",
      "noteEn": "Dar normally means to give. In dar paso a, it forms the conventional expression for allowing someone or something to follow or replace what came before.",
      "example": { "es": "La lluvia da paso al sol.", "en": "The rain gives way to sunshine." },
      "tags": ["word", "dar paso a", "change", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-paso--fixed-dar-paso-a",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "paso",
      "answerEn": "way; passage (as part of dar paso a)",
      "noteEn": "Paso can mean a step or passage. Together with dar and a, it forms dar paso a, “to give way to” or “to make way for.”",
      "example": { "es": "La noche da paso al día.", "en": "Night gives way to day." },
      "tags": ["word", "dar paso a", "fixed expression", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-tienes--age",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tienes",
      "answerEn": "you are (stating age); literally, you have",
      "noteEn": "Spanish states age with tener, “to have.” Tienes is the present form for one person addressed informally, so tienes trece años means that the listener is thirteen years old.",
      "example": { "es": "Tienes veinte años.", "en": "You are twenty years old." },
      "tags": ["word", "tener", "age", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-trece",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "trece",
      "answerEn": "thirteen",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "number", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-es--impersonal-time",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "es",
      "answerEn": "it is (in an impersonal time expression)",
      "noteEn": "Spanish can use es without a separate subject word in expressions such as es hora, “it is time.” English supplies the impersonal word “it.”",
      "example": { "es": "Es hora de salir.", "en": "It is time to leave." },
      "tags": ["word", "ser", "time expression", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-hora--time-to-act",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "hora",
      "answerEn": "time; the moment to act",
      "noteEn": "After es, hora can identify the appropriate moment for an action rather than a clock hour.",
      "example": { "es": "Es hora de empezar.", "en": "It is time to begin." },
      "tags": ["word", "time", "action", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-de--fixed-es-hora-de-que",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "de",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; part of es hora de que",
      "noteEn": "De is the required link between hora and que when the following group of words has its own action and names who should act.",
      "example": { "es": "Es hora de que Marta vuelva.", "en": "It is time for Marta to return." },
      "tags": ["word", "es hora de que", "fixed link", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-que--fixed-es-hora-de-que",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; completes es hora de que before the action",
      "noteEn": "Que completes the time-to-act expression before a group of words with its own action. The following verb uses a special form because the action is called for rather than reported as a fact.",
      "example": { "es": "Es hora de que Marta vuelva.", "en": "It is time for Marta to return." },
      "tags": ["word", "es hora de que", "clause link", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-acepte--accept-subjunctive",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "acepte",
      "answerEn": "I accept (special form for an action that is called for)",
      "noteEn": "Acepte is a form of aceptar used after a trigger such as es hora de que. The ordinary statement form for the speaker, acepto, changes to acepte because the acceptance is being urged or treated as pending, not stated as a fact. The same written form can also match he, she, or formal you; context identifies the speaker here.",
      "example": { "es": "Es hora de que yo acepte el resultado.", "en": "It is time for me to accept the result." },
      "tags": ["word", "aceptar", "subjunctive", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-darle-a-actividad",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "darle a + actividad",
      "questionEn": "How do you colloquially say that someone does, practices, or is into an activity?",
      "answerEn": "Use a matching form of darle, then a, then name the activity.",
      "noteEn": "In this informal pattern, dar changes for the person and time, while the attached le and the following a remain. The activity may include a small word such as el or la: darle al ajedrez, darle a la fotografía. This expression is common in conversation but is less neutral than simply naming the activity with hacer, practicar, or jugar.",
      "example": { "es": "Mis amigos le dan al ciclismo.", "en": "My friends are into cycling." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "activity", "colloquial", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-resultar-adjetivo-a-alguien",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "algo + me/te/le/nos/os/les resulta + adjetivo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone finds something easy, difficult, strange, or similar?",
      "answerEn": "Name the thing, add me, te, le, nos, os, or les for the person, then use a form of resultar and a describing word.",
      "noteEn": "The thing being judged controls the form of resultar: la tarea me resulta fácil, las tareas me resultan fáciles. The short word before resultar identifies the person who experiences the judgment. English often makes that person the subject and says “I find...” or “it seems... to me.” An adjective is a word such as fácil or extraño that describes the thing.",
      "example": { "es": "Estas instrucciones nos resultan claras.", "en": "We find these instructions clear." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "resultar", "experiencer", "evaluation", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-echarse-a-un-lado",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "echarse a un lado",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone steps aside or gets out of the way?",
      "answerEn": "Use a matching form of echarse followed by a un lado.",
      "noteEn": "Echar changes for the person and time, and the attached short word changes with the person: me echo, te echas, se echa, nos echamos. A un lado stays fixed. The expression can describe a physical move or, figuratively, letting someone or something else take over.",
      "example": { "es": "Nos echamos a un lado para dejarlos pasar.", "en": "We stepped aside to let them pass." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "movement", "figurative", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-dar-paso-a",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "dar paso a + persona o cosa",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that one thing gives way to or makes way for another?",
      "answerEn": "Use a matching form of dar, then paso a, then name what follows or replaces it.",
      "noteEn": "Only dar changes for the person and time; paso a stays together. Before el, a and el combine as al: dar paso al invierno. The expression can describe a literal passage or a change in which something new follows.",
      "example": { "es": "El invierno da paso a la primavera.", "en": "Winter gives way to spring." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "change", "sequence", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-tener-edad-en-anos",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "tener + número + años",
      "questionEn": "How do you state someone's age in years?",
      "answerEn": "Use a matching form of tener, then the number, then años.",
      "noteEn": "Spanish describes age as years that a person “has,” so tener changes to match the person: tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tenéis, tienen. English instead uses “to be.” Año becomes singular only for exactly one year: tiene un año.",
      "example": { "es": "Lucía tiene veinte años.", "en": "Lucía is twenty years old." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "tener", "age", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-es-hora-de-que-subjunctive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "es hora de que + acción pendiente",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that it is time for someone to do something?",
      "answerEn": "Use es hora de que, then put the action in the special form used for something that should now happen.",
      "noteEn": "Es hora de que introduces an action as due or called for, not as a reported fact. Spanish therefore uses a special verb form called the subjunctive. For a regular -ar verb, the ordinary ending switches to e: trabaja becomes trabaje and acepta becomes acepte. The action verb still changes to match the person who should act. When no separate doer needs to be named, Spanish can instead use es hora de plus the unchanged dictionary form: es hora de salir.",
      "example": { "es": "Es hora de que Marta trabaje.", "en": "It is time for Marta to work." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "time", "subjunctive", "B1"]
    }
  ],
